Ouster LiDAR sensors support various operating modes which are essentially different configurations of resolution and range. As of my knowledge cutoff in September 2021, Ouster's OS series of LiDAR sensors have the following operating modes:

    Near Field (1024x10): In this mode, the sensor operates at a 1024 x 10 resolution with a 45 meter range, making it suitable for environments requiring high resolution at shorter ranges.

    Standard (512x10): This is the default operating mode for most applications, providing a balance between resolution and range. In this mode, the sensor operates at a 512 x 10 resolution with a 120 meter range.

    Mid Range (512x20): This mode doubles the vertical resolution compared to the standard mode, operating at a 512 x 20 resolution with an 85 meter range. This can be useful for environments requiring more vertical detail.

    Far Field (1024x20): This mode provides the highest resolution and range, operating at a 1024 x 20 resolution with a 120 meter range. This mode may be useful in environments requiring high detail over a long range, but it also consumes the most power and produces the most data.

    Single Return (2048x10): In this mode, the sensor operates at a 2048 x 10 resolution with a 45 meter range. It only reports the first return for each pulse, which may be useful in certain environments.

Each mode is designed for specific use cases, with a trade-off between resolution, range, power consumption, and data output.

The specific details and available modes may have changed if you are viewing this response after September 2021. You should refer to the latest Ouster documentation or contact Ouster support for the most accurate and up-to-date information.



